D={
"John Locke":"John Locke (1632–1704), an English physician and philosopher, lived in exile in the Netherlands for political reasons and returned after the Glorious Revolution of 1688. In Two Treatises of Government he argued that people have natural rights to life, liberty and property, that governments exist by the consent of the governed, and that people may overthrow a government that violates their rights. In An Essay Concerning Human Understanding he argued the mind is a 'blank slate' shaped by experience. His ideas influenced the US Declaration of Independence. He also helped draft the constitution of the Carolina colony and invested in the slave trade, a contradiction critics note.",
"Niccolò Machiavelli":"Niccolò Machiavelli (1469–1527) served as a diplomat and official of the Florentine Republic until the Medici returned in 1512; he was imprisoned and tortured, then exiled to his farm. There he wrote The Prince (1513), advising rulers to be practical, use deception when necessary and, if they cannot be both, to be feared rather than loved. His Discourses on Livy praised republican government. 'Machiavellian' became a byword for cunning, but scholars see him as a founder of realist political science, often compared with India's Chanakya.",
"Mencius":"Mencius (Mengzi, about 372–289 BCE), the most important Confucian after Confucius, was raised by a famously devoted mother who moved house three times to give him good surroundings. He travelled among the Warring States advising rulers. He taught that human nature is good, with innate sprouts of compassion, shame, respect and right and wrong, which must be cultivated. He argued that the people are most important and that a tyrant who oppresses them loses the Mandate of Heaven and may be overthrown. The Mencius became one of the Four Books of the Confucian canon.",
"Nagarjuna":"Nagarjuna (about 150–250 CE) was an Indian Buddhist philosopher, traditionally associated with the Satavahana kings and the region of Nagarjunakonda in Andhra Pradesh. In his Mulamadhyamakakarika he founded the Madhyamaka ('Middle Way') school, arguing that all things are empty (shunya) of independent existence because they arise dependently. He used logic to show that fixed views lead to contradictions. He is revered as a 'second Buddha' in Tibetan and East Asian Buddhism.",
"Louis Pasteur":"Louis Pasteur (1822–1895), a French chemist, showed that fermentation and spoilage are caused by microorganisms, disproving spontaneous generation with his swan-neck flask experiment. He developed pasteurisation to keep wine and milk safe, saved the silk industry from silkworm disease, and helped establish the germ theory of disease. He developed vaccines for chicken cholera, anthrax and, in 1885, rabies, first used on a boy bitten by a rabid dog. The Pasteur Institute he founded continues research; the Pasteur Institute of India in Coonoor was founded in 1907.",
"Thomas Edison":"Thomas Edison (1847–1931) had little formal schooling and worked as a telegraph operator before becoming a full-time inventor. At his Menlo Park laboratory, the first industrial research lab, he invented the phonograph (1877), developed a long-lasting carbon-filament light bulb (1879), and built the first commercial power station at Pearl Street, New York (1882). He also worked on motion picture cameras. He held 1,093 US patents. His 'war of the currents' with Tesla and Westinghouse, promoting direct current, was lost to alternating current.",
"Nikola Tesla":"Nikola Tesla (1856–1943), a Serbian born in Croatia, emigrated to the US in 1884 and briefly worked for Edison. He invented the induction motor and the polyphase alternating-current system, which George Westinghouse used to power the 1893 Chicago World's Fair and Niagara Falls power station, making AC the global standard. He experimented with radio, wireless power and X-rays. His Wardenclyffe tower for wireless transmission failed for lack of funds, and he died poor in a New York hotel. The SI unit of magnetic field is named after him.",
"Michael Faraday":"Michael Faraday (1791–1867), the son of a blacksmith, had little schooling and learned science while apprenticed to a bookbinder. He became Humphry Davy's assistant at the Royal Institution and discovered electromagnetic rotation (the principle of the electric motor, 1821) and electromagnetic induction (1831), the basis of electric generators and transformers. He also discovered benzene and laws of electrolysis. His idea of fields of force inspired Maxwell's equations. He started the Royal Institution Christmas Lectures for children.",
"Alan Turing":"Alan Turing (1912–1954), a British mathematician, described in 1936 a theoretical 'universal machine' that could compute anything computable, the foundation of computer science. During World War II at Bletchley Park he designed the Bombe machine that broke German Enigma codes, shortening the war. After the war he designed early computers and proposed the Turing test for machine intelligence (1950), and studied pattern formation in biology. Convicted of homosexuality in 1952 and forced to undergo chemical castration, he died of cyanide poisoning in 1954. Britain pardoned him in 2013, and he appears on the £50 note.",
"Stephen Hawking":"Stephen Hawking (1942–2018) was diagnosed with motor neurone disease at 21 and given two years to live, but lived another 55, eventually communicating through a speech computer. With Roger Penrose he showed the universe began in a singularity, and in 1974 predicted that black holes emit radiation (Hawking radiation) because of quantum effects. He held Newton's chair at Cambridge, and his book A Brief History of Time (1988) sold over 25 million copies.",
"Homi J. Bhabha":"Homi Jehangir Bhabha (1909–1966), from a wealthy Parsi family in Bombay, studied at Cambridge and did important work on cosmic rays and particle physics. Trapped in India by World War II, he founded the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (1945) with J. R. D. Tata's support and, with Nehru's backing, the Atomic Energy Commission (1948) and Atomic Energy Establishment at Trombay (renamed BARC). He planned India's three-stage nuclear power programme using thorium. He died in an Air India crash on Mont Blanc in 1966.",
"Vikram Sarabhai":"Vikram Sarabhai (1919–1971), from an Ahmedabad industrial family, studied at Cambridge and founded the Physical Research Laboratory (1947). After Sputnik he convinced the government that space technology could serve development. He founded INCOSPAR (1962), launched India's first sounding rocket from Thumba near Thiruvananthapuram in 1963 (parts carried by bicycle and bullock cart), and founded ISRO in 1969. He also co-founded IIM Ahmedabad. He died suddenly at 52; the Vikram lander of Chandrayaan is named after him.",
"Jagadish Chandra Bose":"Jagadish Chandra Bose (1858–1937), a Bengali physicist and botanist, demonstrated wireless transmission of microwaves in Calcutta in 1895 and invented improved detectors, contributing to radio science; he refused to patent his inventions. He then studied plants, inventing the crescograph to measure tiny growth and showing plants respond to stimuli electrically. He faced discrimination in pay at Presidency College and worked without salary for three years in protest. He founded the Bose Institute in 1917.",
"Christopher Columbus":"Christopher Columbus (1451–1506), a Genoese sailor, believed he could reach Asia by sailing west, underestimating the Earth's size. After rejections by Portugal, Ferdinand and Isabella of Spain sponsored him; on 12 October 1492 his three ships reached the Bahamas, and he went on to Cuba and Hispaniola. On four voyages he explored the Caribbean and Central and South American coasts, still believing he was near Asia. As governor of Hispaniola he enslaved and brutally treated the Taíno, and was arrested in 1500. His voyages began European colonisation of the Americas, with devastating results for Indigenous peoples.",
"Vasco da Gama":"Vasco da Gama (about 1460–1524) commanded the Portuguese fleet that sailed around the Cape of Good Hope, up the East African coast and, with a local pilot from Malindi, across the Arabian Sea to Calicut in May 1498. The Zamorin of Calicut was unimpressed by his gifts, but the spices he brought back were worth 60 times the cost. On his second voyage (1502) he bombarded Calicut and burned a pilgrim ship with hundreds aboard. He returned as Viceroy of India in 1524 and died in Cochin.",
"Ferdinand Magellan":"Ferdinand Magellan (1480–1521), a Portuguese navigator who had served in India and Malacca, sailed for Spain in 1519 with five ships to reach the Spice Islands by sailing west. He suppressed a mutiny, discovered the Strait of Magellan at South America's tip, and crossed the Pacific, which he named, as his crew starved. In the Philippines he was killed in the Battle of Mactan (1521) by the forces of chief Lapulapu. Under Juan Sebastián Elcano, one ship and 18 men returned in 1522, completing the first circumnavigation.",
"Vladimir Lenin":"Vladimir Ulyanov 'Lenin' (1870–1924) became a revolutionary after his elder brother was hanged for plotting to kill Tsar Alexander III. He led the Bolshevik faction, arguing for a disciplined party of professional revolutionaries. After the February Revolution of 1917, Germany let him travel from Switzerland to Russia in a sealed train; in October the Bolsheviks seized power. He made peace with Germany, won the civil war using the Cheka secret police and Red Terror, and introduced the New Economic Policy. He founded the Soviet Union in 1922 and died after strokes in 1924.",
"Mustafa Kemal Atatürk":"Mustafa Kemal (1881–1938), born in Ottoman Salonica, became famous defending Gallipoli against Allied landings in 1915. After the Ottoman defeat he organised the Turkish National Movement from Ankara and won the War of Independence (1919–1922) against Greek and Allied forces. He proclaimed the Republic of Turkey in 1923 and served as its first president, abolishing the sultanate and caliphate, closing religious courts, replacing Arabic script with the Latin alphabet, introducing surnames ('Atatürk' means 'father of the Turks') and giving women the vote (1934). His reforms were imposed in an authoritarian way, including the suppression of Kurdish revolts.",
"Sun Yat-sen":"Sun Yat-sen (1866–1925) was educated in Hawaii and Hong Kong as a doctor. He founded revolutionary societies against the Qing dynasty, staged failed uprisings and raised funds among overseas Chinese. After the Wuchang Uprising of 1911 he was elected provisional president of the Republic of China but soon gave way to general Yuan Shikai. He founded the Kuomintang and allied with the Soviet Union and the Communists. His Three Principles of the People — nationalism, democracy and livelihood — are honoured in both China and Taiwan.",
"Ho Chi Minh":"Ho Chi Minh (1890–1969) left Vietnam in 1911 as a ship's cook and lived in France, the US, Britain, the Soviet Union and China, becoming a founding member of the French Communist Party. In 1941 he returned to lead the Viet Minh against Japanese occupation, and on 2 September 1945 he declared Vietnamese independence, quoting the US Declaration. He fought France until its defeat at Dien Bien Phu (1954), then led North Vietnam against the US-backed South; he died before reunification in 1975, when Saigon was renamed Ho Chi Minh City.",
"Che Guevara":"Ernesto 'Che' Guevara (1928–1967), an Argentine medical student, travelled across Latin America on a motorcycle and was radicalised by poverty and US intervention, including the 1954 coup in Guatemala. In Mexico he joined Fidel Castro, and they launched the guerrilla war that overthrew Batista in 1959. He served as Cuba's industry minister and central bank head, and ordered executions of former regime officials. He visited India in 1959, meeting Nehru. He left Cuba to spread revolution in Congo and Bolivia, where he was captured and executed in 1967; his portrait became a global icon.",
"Fidel Castro":"Fidel Castro (1926–2016), a lawyer, led a failed attack on the Moncada barracks in 1953, then waged a guerrilla war from the Sierra Maestra, taking power on 1 January 1959. He nationalised industries and allied with the Soviet Union, survived the US-backed Bay of Pigs invasion (1961) and many assassination attempts, and brought the world close to nuclear war in the 1962 Missile Crisis. Cuba achieved high literacy and health care but jailed dissidents and saw mass emigration. He ruled until 2006, handing power to his brother Raúl.",
"Harriet Tubman":"Harriet Tubman (about 1822–1913) was born into slavery in Maryland, suffered a severe head injury from an overseer, and escaped to Philadelphia in 1849. She returned about 13 times to lead some 70 enslaved people, including family members, to freedom along the Underground Railroad, never losing a passenger. During the Civil War she served the Union as a scout and spy and guided the Combahee River Raid that freed over 700 people. Later she supported women's suffrage and cared for elderly African Americans.",
"Frederick Douglass":"Frederick Douglass (1818–1895) was born into slavery in Maryland, secretly learned to read, and escaped in 1838 disguised as a sailor. His autobiography, Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass (1845), and powerful speeches — such as 'What to the Slave is the Fourth of July?' — made him the leading Black abolitionist. He advised Lincoln, recruited Black soldiers for the Union, supported women's rights at the Seneca Falls convention, and later served as US minister to Haiti.",
}
